keywords:
  - machine learning
  - embedding compression
  - manifold structure
  - chaotic attractors
  - correlation dimension
  - Lyapunov exponents
  - information theory
  - dynamical systems
  - BERT embeddings
  - archival compression
abstract: >
  Large-scale machine learning systems generate billions of high-dimensional
  embedding vectors (768D for BERT-base), creating multi-terabyte archival
  storage requirements. We exploit the intrinsic low-dimensional manifold
  structure of embedding sequences using tools from dynamical systems theory
  (correlation dimension D₂, Lyapunov exponents λ₁). Our PCA-based differential
  encoding achieves 167–178× compression with 13–16% cosine similarity loss
  on real BERT embeddings from Wikipedia and CC-News corpora. We formally
  explain why GZIP achieves only 6.3% efficiency on low-entropy float deltas
  and provide a complete open-source Rust implementation.
